@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+# Configurable MuJoCo adapter
+
+`forma_core.control.mujoco_adapter.MujocoAdapter` implements the existing
+MachineAdapter boundary. MachineRuntime still owns commands, interpolation,
+leases, watchdog state and ownership. This optional backend uses one MuJoCo
+model/data pair and is exclusively virtual.
+
+Author an instance outside repository source as JSON:
+
+- `MujocoConfiguration.definition`: existing MachineDefinition.
+- `scene`: JSON MJCF tree (`tag`, string `attributes`, `children`).
+- `axes`: exact axis ID → scalar joint name and position-actuator name.
+- `switches`: geometry pair, optional slide joint and minimum travel in metres,
+  debounce seconds, initial state. A rising debounced contact/travel observation
+  toggles the virtual state once. Release is required before another toggle.
+- `attachments`: fixed body/ancestor pairs checked against the model topology.
+- `body_bindings` and `joint_bindings`: semantic IDs mapped to simulator names.
+- Optional `human`: local native HumanIR file, SHA-256 and participant ID.
+  All profile segments/joints must bind, and joint ancestry must match.
+- `asset_hashes`: package-relative assets and SHA-256 hashes. External paths,
+  unpinned assets, includes and plugins are rejected.
+
+MJCF coordinates are metres. Joint limits follow MJCF compiler angle settings;
+actuator control limits are in MuJoCo SI units (radians/metres). MachineDefinition
+axes remain degree/mm. Only unit-gear scalar position servos are accepted.
+Virtual human servos are simulation controls, not physical motor designs or
+biomechanical evidence. Native domain validation does not establish anatomy,
+contact safety, electrical behavior, thermal performance or human-use readiness.
+
+Run a host transcript through the existing controller:
+
+```bash
+python -m forma_core.control.mujoco_run interaction.json runtime-trace.json
+MUJOCO_GL=egl python -m forma_core.control.mujoco_render \
+  configuration.json runtime-trace.json views.json interaction.gif
+```
+
+`RunRequest` has a configuration path, duration, host/sample periods and timed
+move/hold events. The host claims a session, arms it, renews leases and sends
+protocol-v2 commands. It never assigns animation trajectories to simulator qpos.
+IK, when used for planning, produces declarative joint targets before execution.
+The backend reads feedback from integrated MuJoCo state. A host clock jump over
+100 ms faults rather than silently skipping time. Disarmed physics pauses.
+
+The trace includes qpos/qvel/ctrl, feedback, observed contact and travel, switch
+transitions and fixed-attachment transforms. Rendering verifies configuration
+and asset hashes, restores only recorded states, and calls mj_forward for
+kinematic reconstruction; it never calls mj_step or interpolates new states.
+View labels and status panels come from presentation JSON. A render receipt pins
+both the trace and resulting GIF hashes. Contact forces in overlays are recorded
+simulation observations, not reconstructed forces or measured hardware values.
+
+Contact geometry, mass, stiffness, servo gains and joint bounds are model inputs.
+The adapter does not provide thermal feedback, firmware emulation, physiological
+models, generalized collision safety or walking balance. Those claims require
+separate validated domains/evaluators. OI may consume exported poses for spatial
+review; it is not the execution authority.
+
+Test with the optional robot-sim dependency installed:
+
+```bash
+python -m unittest discover -s tests/control -p 'test_*.py'
+```
+
+The new adapter tests use a sliding pusher and spring button, independent of any
+human or wearable benchmark.
